<h2>Guidance for the reader</h2>
<h3>What this is</h3>
<p>A record of what one machine looked like at one moment. It was collected by reading only: nothing on the host was written, and nothing was elevated to collect it.</p>
<h3>What it answers</h3>
<p>Which programs, accounts, rules, services and network facts this host carried at the time named in the header, and, wherever a probe could not read something, why not.</p>
<h3>What it does not answer</h3>
<p>Whether any of this is acceptable. A capture is evidence, not a verdict. It is also not a baseline: nobody has approved it, and it carries no signature. And this page does not verify anything; it shows what the bundle says.</p>
<h3>What to do next</h3>
<p>If this is the state you want to hold the host to, have it approved by a person who is accountable for it:</p>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze baseline approve --capture &lt;bundle&gt; --output &lt;baseline&gt; \
  --reviewer &lt;who&gt; --change-id &lt;ticket&gt; --reason &lt;why&gt; --key &lt;private key&gt;</pre>
<h3>To check the bundle behind this page (integrity of every file against the manifest):</h3>
<pre>skillctl trust-freeze verify --bundle &lt;bundle&gt;</pre>


